WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.660
This is the single most important idea in the chapter, so hold on to it.

2
00:00:08.280 --> 00:00:13.000
Electron flow and ATP synthesis are two separate events linked only by a proton

3
00:00:13.000 --> 00:00:17.680
gradient. On the left, electrons hop downhill through carriers in the inner

4
00:00:17.680 --> 00:00:22.820
membrane, and the big exergonic steps do work by pushing protons from the matrix out

5
00:00:22.820 --> 00:00:24.100
into the intermembrane space.

6
00:00:24.880 --> 00:00:29.240
That accumulation of protons is potential energy, like water behind a dam.

7
00:00:29.960 --> 00:00:35.240
On the right, ATP synthase lets those protons flood back down their gradient, and

8
00:00:35.240 --> 00:00:37.920
that flow drives the phosphorylation of ADP.

9
00:00:37.920 --> 00:00:44.360
Peter Mitchell proposed this in 1961, was met with deep skepticism and won the Nobel

10
00:00:44.360 --> 00:00:45.580
Prize for being right.

11
00:00:46.600 --> 00:00:48.980
Let me show you why electrons move at all.

12
00:00:49.640 --> 00:00:53.500
Every carrier has a reduction potential, plotted here on the vertical axis.

13
00:00:54.340 --> 00:00:59.680
NADH sits high, at a very negative potential, meaning it holds electrons loosely and

14
00:00:59.680 --> 00:01:00.680
donates them readily.

15
00:01:01.580 --> 00:01:06.420
Oxygen sits at the bottom, strongly positive, meaning it grabs electrons hungrily.

16
00:01:07.400 --> 00:01:12.100
Left to themselves, electrons flow from low potential to high potential, from

17
00:01:12.100 --> 00:01:17.280
generous donor to greedy acceptor, and that is why they cascade from NADH down to

18
00:01:17.280 --> 00:01:24.160
oxygen. The total drop is about 1.14 volts, which translates to roughly minus 220

19
00:01:24.160 --> 00:01:25.160
kilojoules per mole.

20
00:01:25.880 --> 00:01:30.540
That released energy is exactly what gets spent pumping protons across the membrane.

21
00:01:31.633 --> 00:01:34.193
Now, we assemble the chain from named parts.

22
00:01:34.933 --> 00:01:40.873
Electrons from NADH enter at Complex I, NADH-Q oxidoreductase, and are handed to

23
00:01:40.873 --> 00:01:46.973
ubiquinone. Complex III, Q-cytochrome c oxidoreductase, passes them to cytochrome c.

24
00:01:46.973 --> 00:01:50.933
Complex IV, cytochrome c oxidase, delivers them to oxygen.

25
00:01:51.573 --> 00:01:53.133
Those three are the proton pumps.

26
00:01:53.833 --> 00:01:54.833
Notice the side door.

27
00:01:55.453 --> 00:02:01.713
Complex II, succinate-Q reductase, feeds electrons from succinate-derived FADH2 into

28
00:02:01.713 --> 00:02:04.313
the Q pool, but it does not pump protons.

29
00:02:05.013 --> 00:02:10.573
That single fact, that Complex II is a feeder and not a pump, explains why FADH2

30
00:02:10.573 --> 00:02:13.233
yields less ATP than NADH.

31
00:02:13.553 --> 00:02:18.093
The reduction potentials on the left confirm the path runs downhill overall, even

32
00:02:18.093 --> 00:02:20.953
though a few hops inside the complexes are slightly uphill.

33
00:02:22.033 --> 00:02:26.613
This is a beautiful piece of experimental logic, and it is a favorite exam scenario,

34
00:02:27.033 --> 00:02:28.433
so reason through it with me.

35
00:02:29.033 --> 00:02:32.993
If you block the chain at one point, everything upstream of the block piles up in

36
00:02:32.993 --> 00:02:37.333
the reduced state because electrons cannot leave, and everything downstream becomes

37
00:02:37.333 --> 00:02:39.573
oxidized because no new electrons arrive.

38
00:02:40.273 --> 00:02:47.153
So with an NADH-linked fuel only, rotenone, blocking Complex I, leaves NADH reduced

39
00:02:47.153 --> 00:02:48.573
and the rest oxidized.

40
00:02:49.333 --> 00:02:54.333
Antimycin A, blocking Complex III, leaves carriers up to cytochrome b reduced.

41
00:02:55.393 --> 00:03:00.393
Cyanide, blocking Complex IV, leaves the entire chain reduced and only oxygen

42
00:03:00.393 --> 00:03:06.073
unreduced. By reading which carriers turn reduced versus oxidized, you can deduce

43
00:03:06.073 --> 00:03:08.213
the exact sequence of electron flow.

44
00:03:09.267 --> 00:03:13.827
Tie the whole chapter together with one image, the mitochondrion as a rechargeable

45
00:03:13.827 --> 00:03:18.947
battery. The electron-transport chain is the charging cable, using the energy of

46
00:03:18.947 --> 00:03:24.147
electron flow to pump protons and build up charge, the proton-motive force, across

47
00:03:24.147 --> 00:03:25.147
the inner membrane.

48
00:03:25.767 --> 00:03:30.847
ATP synthase is the discharge circuit, letting protons flow back down to do useful

49
00:03:30.847 --> 00:03:32.307
work and make ATP.

50
00:03:32.307 --> 00:03:34.507
Now the diseases and drugs make sense.

51
00:03:35.227 --> 00:03:40.127
Cut the charging cable as cyanide does by blocking Complex IV and the battery never

52
00:03:40.127 --> 00:03:45.127
charges. Add an uncoupler, which pokes holes in the membrane, and the battery

53
00:03:45.127 --> 00:03:48.587
short-circuits, draining its charge as heat instead of ATP.
